latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Create a deep copy of the input grid to avoid modifying the original.\n\n2. Iterate through each column of the grid:\n   a. Initialize an empty stack to keep track of colors.\n   b. Scan the column from bottom to top.\n\n3. For each cell in the column:\n   a. If the cell is non-black (color != 0):\n      - Add this color to the top of the stack.\n   b. If the cell is black (color == 0):\n      - If the stack is not empty:\n        * Replace this black cell with the color at the top of the stack.\n      - If the stack is empty:\n        * Leave this cell as black (0).\n\n4. After processing all cells in the column, move to the next column and repeat steps 3-4.\n\n5. Return the modified grid.\n\nThis plan ensures that:\n- Colors extend upwards, filling black cells above them.\n- The order of colors is preserved from bottom to top.\n- Black cells act as barriers, stopping the upward extension of colors below them.\n- Multiple non-adjacent colored cells in a column are handled correctly.\n- The original positions of colored cells are maintained.\n\nThe use of a stack is key here. It allows us to keep track of the colors we've encountered as we move up the column, and always fill with the most recently encountered color (which will be at the top of the stack).\n\nThis approach should work for all grid sizes from 1x1 to 30x30, as it processes each column independently and doesn't make assumptions about the grid size."
